\id EZR Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Esdras
\toc1 The First Book of Esdras.
\toc2 1 Esdras
\toc3 EZR
\mt1 The First Book of Esdras.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE BOOKS OF ESDRAS.
\ip \it ESDRAS\it* a holy Priest and Scribe, of the stock of Aaron, by the line of Eleazar, writeth the \f - \ft The two Books of Esdras and Nehemias are but one in the Hebrew.\f* \f - \ft \+it 1. Esdr. 7.\+it*\f* history of God's people, in, and presently after their captivity in Babylon: which \it Nehemias\it* another godly Priest prosecuteth, whose Book is also called the second of Esdras, because in the Hebrew and Greek they are but one Book, relating the acts of them both. The other two books called \it the third and fourth\it* of Esdras, touching the same matter, \it are\it* not in the Hebrew, nor \it received into\it* \f - \ft The third and fourth are not canonical.\f* \it the Canon of holy Scripture\it*, though the Greek Church hold the third book as Canonical, and placeth it first, because it containeth things done before the other.
\ip In the two here following, which are undoubtedly holy Scripture S. Jerome saith, that \f - \ft This history hath also a spiritual sense.\f* \f - \ft \+it Epist. ad Paulin.\+it*\f* \it Esdras and Nehemias (to wit the Helper, & Comforter from God) restored the Temple, and built the walls of the city\it*; adding that \it all the troop of the people returning into their country, also the description of Priests, Levites, Israelites, Proselytes, and the works of walls and towers divided by several families\it*, aliud in cortice praeferunt, aliud in medulla retinent, \it shew one thing in the bark, keep another thing in the marrow\it*: signifying that this history hath both a literal, and a mystical sense. According to the letter, this first Book sheweth the reduction of God's people from \f - \ft First book divided into two parts.\f* Babylon; In the first six chapters. In the other four, their instruction by Esdras after their return.
\is THE CONTINUANCE OF THE CHURCH AND RELIGION, IN THE FIFTH AGE. From the foundation of the Temple, to the captivity in Babylon. The space of 430. years.
\ip \it ALBEIT\it* there were greater Schisms, Heresies, and more revolts from God's law and service in this fifth age, than in the former: Yet the true Church and Religion continued still, and were no less \f - \ft The Church still visible, and the same faith as before.\f* conspicuous than before. Which being clear and evident, touching many principal Articles, we will here only remit the reader to some special places, for confirmation thereof: neither will we be prolix in declaring other points denied, or called into controversy at this time, by the impugners of Catholic Religion.
\ip \it Belief in one God\it* appeareth plainly in building, adorning, and dedicating the Temple with so \f - \ft One God.\f* great solemnity of the Priests, Levites, and all the Tribes, and particularly by King Salomon's prayer, 3. Reg. 7. & 8. 2. Paral. 2. &c. Also Proverb. 8. Eccle. 12. Isaie 41. 44. 45. The Mystery of \f - \ft Three Persons.\f* the \it B. Trinity\it*, Prover. 12. Isaie 6. 48. 49. Ose. 11. Ioel. 2. \it Of Christ our Redeemer\it*, Isaie. 7. 8. 9. 11. \f - \ft Christ.\f* 28. 53. Ierem. 23. 30. 33. Ezech. 17. 34. 37. Dan. 7. 9. Osee. 6. 11. 14. Ioel. 2. Sophon. 2. Aggai 2. Zachar. 2. &c. \it Sacrifices, Sacraments\it*, & other Rites the same as before. But more frequent \f - \ft Sacrifices.\f* Prophecies, that they \it should be changed into better\it* and perfecter \it by Christ\it*. Prov. 9. Isai. 12. 52. \f - \ft Sacraments to be changed by Christ.\f* 55. 61. In the mean time for more signification of the \it singular virtue of Christ's Sacraments\it*, the effect of \it penitential works\it* is often recorded. For example, wicked \it Achab by hair-cloth, fasting, and\it* other \it humiliation escaped\it* part of his deserved \it punishment\it*, 3. Reg. 21. \it Manasses recovered God's\it* \f - \ft Fruits of penance.\f* \it favour\it*, and \it his temporal Kingdom\it*, 2. Par. 33. Who yet was punished in his posterity. 4. Reg. 23. And the \it Ninivites\it* by such penance \it avoided destruction\it*. Ionae 3. Yea nothing is more frequent in the Prophets than preaching of penance. Isa. 1. 2. 3. 30. Iere. 3. 18. &c. and others, all ascribing the cause of plagues, and afflictions to the want of repentance. And \it false Prophets\it* condemned of error and false doctrine, \it for promising the people peace\it*, and security \it in their sins\it*. Ierem. 14. Lamen. 2. Besides \it abstinence\it* from diverse sorts of meats, counted unclean (Isaiae 66.) and \it ordinary fasts\it*, \f - \ft Abstinence.\f* according to the law, \it other fasts\it* were \it appointed\it* sometimes, upon occasions requiring, not \f - \ft Fasts.\f* only to subdue, and mortify the flesh, but also \it to obtain mercy\it* at God's hands in special distresses. 2. Par. 20. Ioel. 1. 2. Ionae 3. \it Elias fasting forty days\it*, 3. Reg. 19. prefigured \it Christ's fast\it*. Which the Church imitateth in \it Lent of forty days\it*, according to human hability, for the fasts of Christ, \f - \ft Lent.\f* Elias, and Moyses were miraculous.
\ip To the Feasts instituted before, was added the \it Dedication of the Temple\it*. 3. Reg. 7. 2. Par. 3. \f - \ft Feasts.\f*
\ip \it Which was built in Mount Moria\it*, 2. Par. 3. the place \it designed long before\it* for this purpose, when \f - \ft Place of the Temple designed long before.\f* Abraham was directed thither by God, and was there ready to sacrifice his son Isaac. Gen. 22.
\ip Where David also offered sacrifice. 2. Reg. 24, 1. Par. 21.
\ip This being the \it only ordinary place for Sacrifice\it*, there were for other uses of daily prayer, reading, preaching, and hearing the word of God other \it Synagogues built\it* (as it were Parish churches) in \f - \ft Synagogues.\f* great number: in Jerusalem it-self four hundred and four score, and many more in the whole Kingdom, as the Hebrew Traditions testify. Of all which places, especially \it of the Temple\it*, there \f - \ft Sanctuary.\f* was \it venerable respect\it* had. For which cause when Joiada the High Priest gave order to kill A thalia, he suffered it not to be done in the Temple, but commanded first to draw her forth. 4. Reg. 11. 2.
\ip Par. 23. And as peculiar places, so \it special Psalms\it*, and \it Hymns\it* were \it appointed\it* for diverse \f - \ft Set form of prayers.\f* purposes and occasions. 2. Par. 20.
\ip The \it ministry of Angels\it* was very usual in this time. One was \it sent to\it* comfort and direct \it Elias\it* the \f - \ft Ministry of Angels.\f* prophet in his afflictions. 3. Reg. 19. 4. Reg. 1. \it An Angel struck the Assyrians' whole camp\it*, 4. Reg. 19. 2. Par. 32. Also the \it Intercession of Angels is\it* so evident, Tobiae 12. Raphael offering Tobias' prayer to God \it , that Protestants have no other refuge to avoid this point of faith, but by denying the Book to be Canonical Scripture\it*.
\ip \it Honour of\it* other \it Saints, and\it* their \it Intercession\it* is proved \it a Maiori\it*. For so much as honour was \f - \ft Honour and Intercession of Saints.\f* religiously exhibited to spiritual power and excellency, in men yet living in this world. So \it a Noble man adored Elias\it* the Prophet, being far greater than he in civil and worldly respects. 3. Reg. 18.
\ip \it Eliseus\it* also was \it adored by his disciples\it*, not for any worldly authority or eminence, but \it for his spiritual power and superiority\it* amongst them. 4. Reg. 2. Likewise all \it Prophets\it* and \it Priests\it* were \it religiously honoured for their holy and spiritual functions\it*. 3. Reg. 13. Much more Saints are rightly honoured, being immortal and in eternal glory. It appeareth also that \it Elias seven\it* years \it after\it* that \it he was translated\it* from human conversation (when Eliseus was chief Prophet, 4. Reg. 3. which was in or before the eighteenth year of Iosaphat, who reigned five and twenty, 3. Reg. 22.) \it had care of Joram\it*, and his kingdom, \it admonishing him by letters\it* of God's wrath, against him and his people for their sins. 2. Par. 21. And the Scripture saith often, that \it God spared and protected Jerusalem, and the Kingdom of Juda for David's sake\it*. 3. Reg. 11. 15. 4. Reg. 8. 19. 20. 2. Par. 6. 21. Isa. 37. We have \f - \ft Relics.\f* also example of \it Saints' Relics\it* in the \it cloak of Elias.\it* 4. Reg. 2. in \it Eliseus' bones\it*, 4. Reg. 13. and in \it another Prophet's body buried\it* in Bethel. Which Iosias would not suffer to be touched. 4. Reg. 23.
\ip \it Images\it* were conserved \it in the Temple\it*, 3. Reg. 7. as before in the Tabernacle: when idolatry was \f - \ft Images.\f* most destroyed. 3. Reg. 15. 4. Reg. 23. yea an abuse rising of the \it brazen serpent\it*, for which Ezechias \it destroyed it\it*, 4. Reg. 18. yet he touched not the Images of Cherubins in the Temple. Which none but Infidels sought to destroy. And \it Osee\it* the Prophet (ch. 3.) \it bewaileth the want of Theraphim\it* or \it Images\it*, amongst other sacred things, Sacrifice, Altar, and Ephod. Whereby the ancient Rabbins prove very well, that \it Images of Angels\it* (and the same of other Saints) are \it not contrary to the Decalogue\it*, but the images of Idols. \it Good works\it* were \it rewarded\it*, and \it bad punished\it*, 3. Reg. 9. and the whole history \f - \ft Good works meritorious.\f* of this age testifieth the same. Where by the way may be observed, that some \it just men fell from their justice\it*, as Salomon 1. Par. 28. 3. Reg. 11. Joas, 4. Reg. 12. 2. Par. 24. Ozias, 2. Paral. 26. \it Others from wickedness returned to piety\it*, as Manasses 4. Reg. 23. 2. Par. 33. the multitude of the people very often much following the disposition of their Kings. \it Special State of life\it* not commanded \f - \ft Evangelical counsels prefigured.\f* by the law, was \it voluntarily professed\it*, and observed by some Prophets, and their disciples called \it the children of Prophets\it*: Keeping \it particular Rules\it*, and wearing \it distinct habit\it*, 4. Reg. 1. 2. 4. The orders of \it Nazarites\it*, and \it Rechabites\it* instituted before, \it continued\it* still, Amos. 2. Ierem. 35. All which \f - \ft Chastity of clergy men, & religious orders.\f* were very exemplar \it figures of Religious State, and Orders in the new Testament\it*, and perpetual chastity of clergy men embraced by such as follow \it Evangelical counsels\it*, proposed, and not commanded by our Saviour. To which S. Paul likewise exhorteth, though there be no precept \f - \ft \+it Mat. 19. 1. Cor. 7. Act. 5. 1. Tim. 5.\+it*\f* thereof to any, before they bind themselves.
\ip \it Exequies for the dead\it* were continually kept, as the sacred history witnesseth, recording where \f - \ft Solemn Exequies for the dead.\f* and with what solemnity the Kings were buried. Which would be overlong, and needless to recite: the like is also written of some Prophets. 3. Reg. 13. 4. Reg. 23. Holy \it Tobias\it* by example and fatherly admonition \it exhorted\it* his son \it to do works of mercy\it*, not only to the living, but also \it to the dead. Put thy bread and thy wine upon the sepulture of the just\it*. ch. 4. Isaias, ch. 57. as the Jews both understood and practised, \it prayed, that peace be given to the just, in his couch\it*, or resting place after his death. \it Of the general Resurrection\it*, Elias' translation is a figure, who yet living sheweth, that \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5.\+it*\f* God can and will restore all men to life again in their bodies, after death, as he conserveth him, an Enoch in their mortal bodies without corruption. Ezechiel also \it prophesieth of the Resurrection\it* \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* of the dead, applying it mystically to spiritual resurrection, and restoration of Israel to former state. ch. 37. Of the \it last Judgement\it*, and \it eternal glory to\it* the good, and everlasting \it pain\it* to the \f - \ft Judgement.\f* wicked, Salomon agreeably to the doctrine of other Prophets, discourseth in his book of \f - \ft Eternal glory or pain.\f* Ecclesiastes, namely ch. 3. 11. and in the last concludeth thus: \it Let us altogether hear the end of speaking: Fear God, and observe his commandments: for this is every man\it* (or, to this end man is created) \it and God will bring into Judgement all things, that are done, for every error\it* (or obscure thing) \it whether it be good or evil\it*.
\ip Neither were these and other \it points of Faith and Religion\it* interrupted, but \it still believed and\it* \f - \ft Church without interruption.\f* \it professed\it* in the \it Church always visible and incontaminate\it*, notwithstanding \it some boughs\it* and branches became unfruitful, and rotten: others \it brake off and were separated\it* from this vine. For when \it Salomon falling\it* to luxury, multiplying many wives and concubines, was by them seduced and brought \it to spiritual fornication\it* and idolatry, \it making altars, and offering sacrifices to Idols, the Priests, Prophets, and people generally persevered in God's law\it* and service. 3. Reg. 11. After \f - \ft Jeroboam's wicked policy.\f* whose death \it Jeroboam\it* his servant, of the tribe of Ephraim, \it possessing Ten Tribes\it* (called the Kingdom of Israel) to maintain his new state, fearing that if the people resorted to Jerusalem for religion's sake, they would depart from him, and return to the right heirs of David and Salomon, \it made an egregious Schism, setting up two golden calves in Bethel and Dan\it*, 3. Reg. 12. \it made temples, altars, and Priests\it* to serve them, all opposite to God's ordinance. But not only the other \it Two Tribes\it*, called the Kingdom of Juda, but also \it the greatest part of Israel\it*, especially \it Priests, Levites\it*, and \it devoutest people, repaired still to Jerusalem, not yielding to\it* that \it schism\it* and idolatry. 2. Par. 11. Moreover God raised up and \it sent\it* special \it Prophets\it* to confirm the weak and recall the seduced.
\ip For Jeroboam has no sooner set up his new altar in Bethel, and begun to offer incense upon it, \f - \ft Prophets inspired by God to resist Schism and Heresy.\f* but \it a Prophet came out of Juda in the word of our Lord\it*, and cried against that altar, foretelling that whereas for that present they burnt frankincense upon it, the time should come, when the false Priests should be burned there, confirming by present \it miracles\it* that which he averred in words, \f - \ft \+it 4. Reg. 23.\+it*\f* \it the King's hand suddenly withering\it*, and \it restored\it* again \it by the Prophet's prayer\it*, and the new \it altar cleaving\it* in sunder, that the ashes fell out. 3. Reg. 13. Further another Prophet called \it Ahias\it* \f - \ft The often change of Kings, and evil succeed in the Kingdom of Israel.\f* \it foreshewed\it* the destruction and utter \it extirpation of Jeroboam's family\it*, for his enormous wickedness, and namely (which is most often inculcate) \it for making Israel to sin\it*, by devising and setting abroach a new religion. 3. Reg. 14. which ruin happened very shortly. For himself reigning twenty two \f - \ft The first family reigned but 24. years.\f* years (3. Reg. 14.) one of his sons died presently \it according to the Prophet's word\it*. v. 18. Another called \it Nadab\it* succeeding to his father, reigned only two year, and was \it slain\it* together \it with their whole race\it* and kindred, by \it Baasa\it* of the tribe of Issachar. 3. Reg. 15. Likewise Baasa following \f - \ft The second new family. 26.\f* the bad steps of Jeroboam was forewarned by \it Jehu a Prophet\it*, that his house should also be destroyed. And accordingly when he had reigned four and twenty years, his son \it Ela\it* reigned but two years, was \it slain\it* by his servant \it Zambri\it*, and all his kindred destroyed. Which Zambri \f - \ft The third but 7. days.\f* reigned but seven days. For being forthwith besieged by \it Amri\it*, of the tribe of Benjamin, he desperately burned himself together with the King's palace. Neither did Amri then possess the \f - \ft The fourth, 48.\f* Kingdom with peace. For he being chosen King by the army only, whereof he was General, another part of the people chose & followed \it Thebni. Whereof\it* arose \it civil War\it* between the Anti-Kings, continuing three years: till Thebni died, and so Amri reigned alone, but wickedly as his predecessors, twelve years in all. Then succeeded his son \it Achab\it* most wicked. \it Who married Jezabel\it* a Sidonian, & by her was persuaded to worship Baal. 3. Reg. 16. To him notwithstanding God sent many admonitions by sundry Prophets, and bestowed great benefits upon him, whereupon he did some notorious penitential works, but not persevering in any good thing, returned to his wickedness. 3. Reg. 20. And finally \it believing false Prophets\it*, and persecuting Micheas for prophesying the truth, was slain in battle when he thought himself most secure. 3. Reg. 22. having reigned twenty two years. 3. Reg. 16. His son \it Ochozias\it* reigning but two years fell through a window, and died of the hurt. 4. Reg. 1. His other son Joram, after twelve years was \it slain\it* by \it Jehu\it* of another family: who then dispatched \it Jezabel\it*, and leaving her in the street, the dogs did eat \f - \ft The fifth. 103.\f* her carcass. He also caused seventy sons of Joram to be slain, and utterly destroyed all Achab's house. 4. Reg. 10. For which service he was established in the Kingdom, for four generations, v. 30. So himself reigning twenty eight years, 3. Reg. 10. after him reigned successively his son \it Joachaz\it* seventeen years, his son \it Joas\it* sixteen years, 4. Reg. 13. his son \it Jeroboam\it* one and forty years. Lastly his son \it Zacharias\it*, whom his servant \it Sellum\it*, of another race, killed when he had reigned but six months, 4. Reg. 15. And after one month \it Sellum\it* was slain by \it Manahen\it* of another progeny. \f - \ft The sixth, one month.\f*
\ip Who reigned ten years. Then his son \it Phaceia\it* reigning two years, was slain by \it Phacee\it* of \f - \ft The seventh, 12. years.\f* another generation. He reigning twenty years, \it many\it* of his people were \it carried captive into\it* \f - \ft The eighth 20. years.\f* \it Assyria\it*, and himself was slain by \it Osee\it* of another kindred. 4. Reg. 15. Finally \it the Assyrians\it* \f - \ft The ninth nine years.\f* taking Samaria by three years siege, in the ninth year of Osee \it possessed the Kingdom of Israel, and led all the principal persons captives\it* into Assyria: about two hundred forty two years \f - \ft Then overthrown, and the Kingdom never restored.\f* after that Jeroboam first reigned over the Ten Tribes. Thus there were in all \it nineteen Kings. Besides Thebni\it*, who only reigned in part against another. Of which the first Jeroboam, and Jehu were advanced by God's ordinance, for punishment of others. Amri was chosen by the army, the rest of the people choosing Thebni. Six invaded by mere force, killing their predecessors. The rest succeeded, by such titles as their fathers had. And though some were better some worse than others, all were wicked, and at last overthrown.
\ip Contrarywise \it in the Kingdom of Juda\it* standing after the separation of ten tribes about four \f - \ft The Kingdom of Juda for David's sake conserved in his seed.\f* hundred years, though some Kings were wicked, yet some were good, and in them all \it God preserved David's seed, by the line of Salomon\it*, in this direct succession: \it Roboam, Abias, Asa, Iosaphat, Joram, Ochozias, Joas\it* (in whose infancy, his grandmother \it A thalia usurped\it* the Kingdom six years) \it Amasias, Ozias, Joathan, Achaz, Ezechias, Manasses, Amon, Josias, Joachaz\it* (hitherto the son ever succeeding his father) then \it Ioakim\it* (brother of Joachaz) Ioachin otherwise called \it Jechonias\it*, son of Joachaz. And finally his Uncle \it Sedecias\it*, who was carried captive into Babylon. But \it Jechonias by God's special providence\it*, was \it favoured & exalted\it* by a new King of Babylon, whither he was led captive before. \it In whose line David's offspring continued\it* though not with title of Kings, \it yet in eminent state\it*, and estimation. As resteth to be noted in the sixth age of the world.
\ip The \it progeny\it* also \it of Aaron continued\it* in their office and function of Priesthood, \it with succession of\it* \f - \ft Succession of Priests continued.\f* \it High Priests\it*, as before from Aaron to Sadoc, partly in the line of Eleazar, partly of Ithamar, both Aaron's sons, so from Sadoc, by the like succession of both families. For \it of Eleazar\it* is recorded this \it Genealogy\it* 1. Paral. 6. \it Sadoc, Achimaas, Azarias, Johanan, Azarias, Amarias, Achitob, Sadoc, Sellum, Helcias, Azarias, Zaraias\it*, and \it Josedech\it*, who was High Priest in the captivity (v. 15.) being carried into Babylon in the first transmigration with King Jechonias, before the general captivity of all, as it seemeth 4. Reg. 24. his father \it Zaraias\it* yet living, who was \it slain\it* nine years after by Nabuchodonosor, 4. Reg. 25. And amongst these there were some \it High Priests of Ithamar's line\it*. To wit, \it Joram, Joiada\it*, (4. Reg. 11. 2. Par. 23.) \it Joathan, Urias\it*, (4. Reg. 16.) and some others; or else some of the above mentioned, had other names, recited by Iosephus lib. 10. cap. 11. Antiq. and Nicephorus lib. 2. cap. 4. Hist. Eccles.
\ip Moreover besides this ordinary succession of Priests, there was an \it extraordinary mission of\it* \f - \ft Extraordinary mission of Prophets.\f* \it Prophets\it*: to supply more fully the office of preaching the truth, and admonishing offenders. And these God \it inspired\it* and sent, most especially \it when and where errors sprung, and sins most abounded\it*: giving them \it extraordinary grace\it* and most excellent virtues, to counterpoise the enormities of wicked men. Such were in the times of Achab and Jezabel, in the kingdom of Israel, besides many others, the \it two famous great Prophets Elias, & Eliseus\it*. Whose admirable lives and holy \f - \ft Great effects of their preaching and miracles.\f* conversation were a mirror to the world, and great terror to the wicked, whose works and miracles marvelously confirmed the well disposed, encouraged the weak, converted many transgressors, confounded false Prophets, justified their own preaching, and much glorified God. \it Elias 1. shut\it* \f - \ft Elias his miracles.\f* \it the heaven, that it rained not\it* in three years. \it 2. was fed by ravens. 3. Multiplied a poor widow's meal and oil. 4. Raised her dead son to life\it* (3. Reg. 17.) \it 5. Brought fire from heaven, to burn his sacrifice\it*: thereby confounding four hundred and fifty false prophets of Baal. \it 6.\it* by prayer \it procured rain.\it* (3. Reg. 18.) \it 7. Fasted\it* without eating or drinking \it forty days\it* and nights together. (3. Reg. 19.) \it 8. Procured fire\it* from heaven, \it which devoured two\it* insolent \it captains, and their hundred men\it* (4. Reg. 1.) \it 9. Divided\it* the river of \it Jordan\it* with his cloak, that himself and Eliseus pass over the dry channel. \it 10. Was assumpted in a fiery chariot\it* into some place, where he yet liveth. And parting away \it obtained\it* of God the like \it double spirit\it* (of prophecy and miracles) to Eliseus. In like manner \it Eliseus 1.\it* \f - \ft Eliseus his miracles.\f* \it divided Jordan again by Elias' cloak\it*, and so returned to his disciples. \it 2. Amended\it* the bitterness of certain \it waters, by casting in salt. 3. Boys\it* being \it cursed by him\it*, for deriding him, \it were\it* forthwith torn by bears (4. Reg. 2.) \it 4.\it* He \it procured water without rain\it* for three Kings in the camp (4. Reg. 3.) \it 5. Multiplied\it* a poor widow's \it oil. 6. By his prayers a barren woman became fruitful. 7.\it* He \it raised\it* her son \it from death. 8. Made\it* the \it bitter broth\it* of his disciples \it sweet. 9. Fed many with few loaves\it* (4. Reg. 4.) \it 10. Cured Naaman of leprosy. 11. Struck Giezi with the same\it* (4. Reg. 5.) \it 12. Made iron to swim. 13. Knew the secret counsels\it* of the Syrian \it King. 14. Made one see horse-men, and fiery chariots, which to others were invisible. 15. Made the Syrians blind\it*, that were sent to apprehend him, and so led them into Samaria. \it 16. Foreshewed unexpected plenty of corn\it* the next day, with the \it death of\it* a great \it man, that would not believe it\it* (4. Reg. 7.) \it 17.\it* And after his death \it another man's dead body touching his bones was revived.\it* 4. Reg. 13.
\ip Other Prophets wrought also miracles, but these for examples may suffice to shew, that \it God\it* \f - \ft Religion not wholly destroyed in the Kingdom of Israel.\f* \it preserved religion also in the Kingdom of Israel\it*. Which himself further testified, even in most desolate times, when Elias lamented, that he was left alone (3. Reg. 19.) For God answered, \it that seven thousand\it* (meaning thereby a great multitude) \it had not bowed their knees to Baal\it*, not so much as in outward shew conformed themselves to infidelity, or idolatry. \it Jehu\it* in his time, destroyed all the \it worshippers of Baal.\it* (4. Reg. 10.) But \it none\it* at any time \it could wholly destroy true Israelites\it*: For \it God would not suffer it.\it* 4. Reg. 14. v. 27.
\ip Yea notwithstanding \it diverse notorious heresies\it* were preached, & followed \it in that Kingdom of\it* \f - \ft Heresies in the Kingdom of Israel.\f* \it the Ten tribes\it*, yet all did not fall, nor embrace them. \it Jeroboam\it* not only made and set up golden calves, but also \it taught that\it* they were Gods, saying: \it Behold\it* thy Gods, O Israel, which \it brought\it* \f - \ft Jeroboamites.\f* \it thee out of the land of Aegypt\it*. 3. Reg. 12. \it making temples, altars\it*, and \it imaginary Priests, which were not of the children of Levi\it*. Also \it a feast the fifteenth day of the month, after the similitude of the solemnity, that was celebrated in Juda\it*. All which the holy Scripture saith: \it He forged of his own heart\it*. That very property of Arch-heretics. But the \it true Priests, Levites, &\it* many \it others, that had\it* \f - \ft Many constant in true religion.\f* \it given their heart to seek our Lord, went into Jerusalem, to immolate their victims before our Lord the God of their fathers\it*. 2. Par. 11. Yea \it Naaman\it* a stranger of Syria, and a Neophyte in religion, \it taught\it* by his example, \it that none may yield conformity, nor otherwise communicate with Infidels, than God's Priests and Prophets approve for lawful\it*. 4. Reg. 5.
\ip Unto this heresy of Jeroboam, \it Achab by Jezabel's persuasion added the worshipping of Baal\it* as God, \f - \ft Jezabelites.\f* 3. Reg. 16. making both temple and altar to him in Samaria. Jeroboam's priests serving fitly this purpose. Though all the former heretics no more agreed to this new heresy, than Lutherans now admit of Calvinism. For \it Jehu a Jeroboamite destroyed all Jezabelites\it* that he could by a stratagem get together. 4. Reg. 10. v. 28. 29. Much less did all Israel serve Baal.
\ip Again, after that Salmanazar King of Assyria had taken Samaria, and placed there a new \f - \ft Samaritanites, divided into many Sects.\f* people, 4. Reg. 17. they learning the rites of the Israelites' \it religion, mixed\it* their \it Paganism\it* therewith, and \it made a new heresy\it*, or rather \it many new heresies\it*. For being \it diverse nations\it* they \it had\it* in several conventicles their \it particular gods\it*, and \it so many diverse Sects\it*. The \it Babylonians, Cutheites, Emathites, Heveites\it*, and \it Sapharvaimites\it*. 4. Reg. 17. But as the Priests, which taught them rites of true religion, allowed not of this mixture, so doubtless some people harkened to their admonitions, and kept religion simply and sincerely. And at this very time of the Ten tribes' captivity, \it holy Tobias\it* \f - \ft Tobias never yielded to Schism.\f* who was carried captive with the rest, \it neither before nor after the captivity, left the law of God. But went to Jerusalem\it* (when others served Jeroboam's golden calves) to the Temple of our Lord, and there adored the Lord God of Israel. And in captivity bestowed himself in works of mercy towards the living and dead of his nation. Tob. 1.
\ip As for \it the Kingdom of Juda\it*, it was \it more free from heresies\it*. For very few or none of those Kings \f - \ft The Kingdom of Juda more free from heresy.\f* that fell to other gross enormities, yea to manifest idolatry, became heretics, as is probably collected by that Isaias the Prophet being sent to Achaz, admonished him, conversed and dealt with him, as with one that believed wholly and solidly true religion: assuring him that God would protect Jerusalem, bidding him not to \it fear the two smoking firebrands, in the wrath of Rasin King of Syria, and of Phacee King of Israel\it*. Isa. 7. Further bidding him ask a sign of God, he answered, \f - \ft King Achaz.\f* though frowardly, yet not as an infidel: \it I will not ask: and I will not tempt our Lord\it*. Yea though \f - \ft Urias high Priest.\f* \it Urias\it* the \it High Priest\it* by commandment of the same King (4. Reg. 16.) \it made a new altar\it* in place of God's Altar, \it yet\it* he \it erred not in faith\it*, nor in doctrine, as teaching in Moyses chair, \it but in fact\it* only, and of frailty for fear of the King, as the King offended in his external act, to flatter the King of Syria. And in this case God sent Isaias to admonish the King, which Urias neglected, or durst not do. Likewise \it Joram\it* (4. Reg. 8. 2. Par. 21.) \it Ochozias\it* (2. Par. 22.) \it Joas\it* in the later part of his life, \f - \ft King Joram and others committing idolatry in fact, many others still professed true Religion.\f* 2. Par. 24. \it Manasses\it* in the former part of his reign (4. Reg. 2. 2. Par. 33.) and some other Kings of Juda committing idolatry, and making others to fall with them, either were \it not wholly perverted\it*, or at least \it drew not all with them\it*. For not only \it Prophets, in whose hand\it* (or ministry) \it God spake\it*, and reproved these sins, but many others kept their Zeal of true religion, as appeared in their promptness to serve God, when by good Kings Asa, Josaphat, Ezechias, Iosias, and others, they were exhorted, or admitted so to do. 4. Reg. 18. 23. 2. Par. 15. 17. 29. 30. 31. 33. 34. &c.
\ip Finally, whereas diverse \it good Princes disposed, things belonging to Divine service\it* in the temple, \it correcting faults\it*, and \it punishing offenders\it* in that behalf (3. Reg. 15. 4. Reg. 18. 23.) they \it did the same without prejudice of the High Priest's supremacy\it* in spiritual causes, and their godly acts \it make nothing for the English Paradox of Layheadship\it*. For superior authority and ordinary power is \f - \ft Authority depending upon divine ordinance, is not changed by facts or practice.\f* not proved by facts good or evil, but rather by God's ordinance and institution. For as the facts of usurpers make no lawful prescription, so neither the facts of good men, do change God's general ordinance and law: But are done either by way of execution, or sometimes by dispensation. Often also by commission and special inspiration of God. As King David by dispensation did eat \f - \ft Good Kings defended and promoted religion not as chief in spiritual causes, but by way of execution, dispensation, or commission. Priests by their negligence do sin but lose not their authority.\f* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 12.\+it*\f* the holy bread, which was ordained for Priests only. 1. Reg. 21. He disposed of Priests' and Levites' offices about the Ark of God. Par. 15. 19. by way of execution according to the law. And of the like offices in the Temple (when it should be built) 1. Par. 23. 24. 25. 26. by divine inspiration. And Salomon by commission from God deposed Abiathar the High Priest from his office, and put Sadoc in his place. 3. Reg. 2. Wherefore albeit good Kings did excellently well in calling together the Priests, and disposing them in their offices, for execution of God's service, yea in commanding what they should do 4. Reg. 18. 19. 22. and in punishing Priests (4. Reg. 23.) yet they did such things as \it God's Commissioners\it*, not as ordinary Superiors \it in spiritual causes\it*, and still the ordinary subordination made by the law, Deut. 17. Num. 27. stood firm and inviolable, the \it High Priest supreme Judge\it* of all doubts in faith, causes, and quarrels in religion, when other subordinate inferior Judges varied in their judgements. Of which offices Malachias the Prophet: (c. 2.) admonisheth Priests in his time, that whereas they were negligent, not performing their duty, their sin was the greater, for that their authority still remained, and the perpetual Rule of the law, that \it the lips of the Priest shall keep knowledge, and they\it* (other men generally) \it shall require the law of his mouth, because he is the Angel of the Lord of hosts\it*. And all Princes & others \it were to receive the\it* \f - \ft \+it Deut. 17. v. 18.\+it*\f* \it law at the Priest's hand\it* of the Levitical Tribe. This was the warrant of stability in truth of the Synagogue in the old Testament. Much more \it the Church\it* and Spouse \it of Christ\it*, whose \f - \ft The Church of the old Testament conserved in truth. Much more the Church of Christ.\f* excellency and singular privileges \it Salomon describeth in his canticle of canticles\it*, hath such \f - \ft \+it in Psal. 30. conc. 2. li. 3. c. 32. doctrine Christ. 1. Tim. 3. 4. Reg. 19.\+it*\f* warrant. \it Of this spouse all the Prophets writ\it*, & that more plainly than of Christ himself, \it foreseeing more adversaries bending their forces against her\it*, as S. Augustine observeth, \it than against Christ\it* her head. And the same holy father in many places teacheth, that she \it neither perisheth nor loseth her beauty\it* for the mixture of evil members, in respect of whom she \it is black, but fair\it* in respect of the good, Cantic. 1. Notwithstanding therefore sinners remaining within the Church, schismatics and heretics breaking from the Church, still she remaineth the \it pillar and firmament of truth, the virgin daughter of Sion\it*.
\c 1
\cd Cyrus king of Persia moved by divine inspiration, releaseth God's people from captivity, with license to return and build the Temple in Jerusalem: 7. restoring the holy vessels, which Nabuchodonosor had taken from thence.
\p
\v 1 In \x - \xt Isa. 44. 45. Jer. 25. 29.\x*\f 2 \fr 1.1 \ft The first part. The return of God's people from Babylon.\f* the first year of Cyrus king of the Persians, that the word of our Lord by the mouth of Jeremy might be accomplished, our Lord raised up the spirit of Cyrus king of the Persians: & he made proclamation in all his Kingdom, yea by writing, saying:
\v 2 Thus saith Cyrus king of the Persians: All the Kingdoms of the earth hath the Lord the God of heaven given me, and he hath commanded me that I should build him a house in Jerusalem, which is in Jewry.
\v 3 Who is there among you of all his people? His God be with him. Let him go up into Jerusalem, which is in Jewry, and build the house of the Lord the God of Israel, he is the God that is in Jerusalem.
\v 4 And let all the rest in all places wheresoever they dwell, let every man of his place help him, with silver and gold, and substance, and cattle, besides that which they offer voluntarily to the temple of God, which is in Jerusalem.
\v 5 And there rose up the Princes of the fathers of Juda and Benjamin, and the Priests, and Levites, and every one, whose spirit God raised up, to go up to build the temple of our Lord, which was in Jerusalem.
\v 6 And all that were round about, \f a \fr 1.6 \ft Liberally gave such things into their hands.\f* did help their hands in vessels of silver & of gold, in substance, and beasts, in furniture, besides those things which they had offered voluntarily.
\v 7 King Cyrus also brought forth the vessels of the temple of our Lord, which Nabuchodonosor had taken out of Jerusalem, & had put them in the temple of his god.
\v 8 But Cyrus the king of Persians brought them forth by the hand of Mithridates the son of Gazabar, & numbered them to Sassabasar the Prince of Juda.
\v 9 And this is the number of them: Phials of gold thirty, Phials of silver a thousand, knives twenty nine, goblets of gold thirty,
\v 10 goblets of silver of the second order, four hundred ten: other vessels a thousand.
\v 11 All the vessels of gold & silver, five thousand four hundred: Sassabasar took all, with them that went up from the transmigration of Babylon into Jerusalem.
\c 2
\cd The names and number of special men, which returned under the conduct of Zorobabel into Jerusalem: 66. their substance of cattle, 68. and their oblations for the re-edifying of the Temple.
\p
\v 1 And \f a \fr 2.1 \ft This enumeration of the Israelites, which ascended into Jerusalem, signifieth the Elect which ascend from the militant Church to the triumphant.\f* these are the children of the province that went up from the captivity, which Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon had transported into Babylon, and returned into Jerusalem and Juda, every man into his city.
\v 2 They that came with Zorobabel, Josue, Nehemia, Saraia, Rahelaia, Mardochai, Belsan, Mesphar, Beguai, Rehum, Baana: The number of the men of the people of Israel:
\v 3 The children of Pharos two thousand an hundred seventy two.
\v 4 The children of Sephatia, three hundred seventy two.
\v 5 The children of Area, seven hundred seventy five.
\v 6 The children of Phahath Moab, of the children of Josue: Joab, two thousand eight hundred twelve.
\v 7 The children of Ælam a thousand two hundred fifty four.
\v 8 The children of Zethua, nine hundred forty five.
\v 9 The children of Zachai, seven hundred sixty.
\v 10 The children of Bani six hundred forty two.
\v 11 The children of Bebai, six hundred twenty three.
\v 12 The children of Azgad, a thousand two hundred twenty two.
\v 13 The children of Adonicam, six hundred sixty six.
\v 14 The children of Beguai, two thousand fifty six.
\v 15 The children of Adin, four hundred fifty four.
\v 16 The children of Ather, which were of Ezechias, ninety eight.
\v 17 The children of Besai, three hundred and twenty three.
\v 18 The children of Iora, an hundred twelve.
\v 19 The children of Hasum, two hundred twenty three.
\v 20 The children of Gebbar, ninety five.
\v 21 The children of Bethlehem, an hundred twenty three.
\v 22 The men of Netupha, fifty six.
\v 23 The men of Anathoth an hundred twenty eight.
\v 24 The children of Azmaveth, forty two.
\v 25 The children of Cariathiarim, Cephira, & Beroth, seven hundred forty three.
\v 26 The children of Rama and Gabaa, six hundred twenty one.
\v 27 The men of Machmas, an hundred twenty two.
\v 28 The men of Bethel and Hai, two hundred twenty three.
\v 29 The children of Nebo, fifty two.
\v 30 The children of Megbis, an hundred fifty six.
\v 31 The children of another Ælam, a thousand two hundred fifty four.
\v 32 The children of Harim, three hundred twenty.
\v 33 The children of Lod Hadid and Ono, seven hundred twenty five.
\v 34 The children of Jericho, three hundred forty five.
\v 35 The children of Senaa, three thousand six hundred thirty.
\v 36 The Priests: The children of Iadaia in the house of Josue, nine hundred seventy three.
\v 37 The children of Emmer, a thousand fifty two.
\v 38 The children of Pheshur, a thousand two hundred forty seven.
\v 39 The children of Harim, a thousand and seventeen.
\v 40 The Levites: The children of Josue and Cedmiel, the children of Odovia, seventy four.
\v 41 The singing men: The children of Asaph, an hundred twenty eight.
\v 42 The children of the Porters: the children of Sellum, the children of Ater, the children of Telmon, the children of Accub, the children of Hatita, the children of Sobai: all an hundred thirty nine.
\v 43 The Natheneites: The children of Siha, the children of Hasupha, the children of Tabbaoth,
\v 44 The children of Ceros, the children of Siaa, the children of Phadon,
\v 45 The children of Lebana, the children of Hagaba, the children of Accub,
\v 46 The children of Hagab, the children of Semlai, the children of Hanan,
\v 47 The children of Gaddel, the children of Gaher, the children of Raaia,
\v 48 The children of Rasin, the children of Necoda, the children of Gazam,
\v 49 The children of Aza, the children of Phasea, the children of Besee,
\v 50 The children of Asena, the children of Munim, the children of Nephusim,
\v 51 The children of Bacbuc, the children of Hacupha, the children of Harhur,
\v 52 The children of Besluth, the children of Mahida, the children of Harsa,
\v 53 The children of Bercos, the children of Sisara, the children of Thema,
\v 54 The children of Nasia, the children of Hatipha,
\v 55 The children of the servants of Salomon, the children of Sotai, the children of Sophereth, the children of Pharuda,
\v 56 The children of Iala, the children of Dercon, the children of Geddel,
\v 57 The children of Saphatia, the children of Hatil, the children of Phochereth, which were of Asebaim, the children of Ami.
\v 58 All the Nathinaites, & the children of the servants of Salomon, three hundred ninety two.
\v 59 And these are they that came up from Thelmela, Thelharsa, Cherub, and Adon, and Emer. And they could not shew the house of their fathers and their seed, whether they were of Israel.
\v 60 The children of Dalaia, the children of Tobia, the children of Necoda, six hundred fifty two.
\v 61 And of the children of the Priests: The children of Hobia, the children of Accos, the children of Berzellai, who took a wife of the daughters of Berzellai the Galaadite, and was called by their name:
\v 62 these sought the writing of their genealogy, and \f b \fr 2.62 \ft Such as say they are Priests and cannot shew their vocation, must not exercise that function.\f* found it not, and they were cast out of the Priesthood.
\v 63 And Athersatha said to them, that they should not eat of the Holy of holies, till there rose a Priest learned and perfect.
\v 64 All the multitude as it were one man, \f c \fr 2.64 \ft All above numbered of the tribes of Juda, Benjamin, & Levi, do not amount to 30. thousand three hundred. So in this general number are contained above twelve thousand of other tribes, not recited among the rest, as Rabbi Salomon explicateth the difficulty.\f* forty two thousand three hundred sixty:
\v 65 beside their men servants, and women servants, which were seven thousand three hundred thirty seven: & among them singing men, & singing women two hundred.
\v 66 Their horses seven hundred thirty six, their mules two hundred forty five,
\v 67 their camels, four hundred thirty five, their asses six thousand seven hundred twenty.
\v 68 And of the Princes of the fathers, when they entered into the temple of our Lord, which is in Jerusalem, they offered voluntarily unto the house of our Lord to build it in his place.
\v 69 According to their abilities, they gave the expenses of the work, of gold sixty one thousand solids, of silver five thousand pounds, & garments for the Priests an hundred.
\v 70 The Priests therefore and the Levites, and they of the people, and the singing men, and the porters, and the Nathinaites dwelt in their cities, and all Israel in their cities.
\c 3
\cd An Altar is built for sacrifice: 4. The feast of Tabernacles solemnly celebrated. 8. And in the second year (after their return) the Temple is founded with great joy of the people, and mourning of some.
\p
\v 1 And now the seventh month was come, and the children of Israel were in their cities: the people therefore was gathered together as it were one man into Jerusalem.
\v 2 And Josue the son of Josedec rose up, and his brethren the Priests, and Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and his brethren, and they built the altar of the God of Israel, that they might offer on it holocausts, as it is written in the law of Moyses the man of God.
\v 3 And they placed the altar of God upon his feet, the people of the lands round about putting them \f a \fr 3.3 \ft Notwithstanding the terror of infidels, God's servants took courage to offer sacrifice.\f* in fear, and they offered upon it holocaust to our Lord morning and evening.
\v 4 And they made the solemnity of tabernacles, as it is written, and holocaust every day by order according to the precept, the work of the day in his day.
\v 5 And after these things the continual holocaust, as well in the Calends, as in all the solemnities of our Lord, that were consecrated, and in all wherein there was offered voluntarily a gift to our Lord.
\v 6 From the first day of the seventh month they began to offer holocaust to our Lord: moreover the temple of God was not yet founded.
\v 7 And they gave money to hewers of stones and to masons: meat also and drink, and oil to the Sidonians & Tyrians, that they should bring cedar trees from Libanus to the sea unto Joppe, according to that which Cyrus the king of the Persians had commanded them.
\v 8 And in the second year of their coming to the temple of God in Jerusalem, the second month, began Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and Josue the son of Josedec, and the rest of their brethren the Priests, and the Levites, and all that were come from the captivity into Jerusalem, & they appointed Levites from twenty years and upward, that they should hasten forward the work of our Lord.
\v 9 And Josue stood and his sons, and his brethren, Cedmihel, and his sons, and the children of Juda, as it were one man, that they might be instant upon them, that did the work in the temple of God: the sons of Henadad, and their sons, & their brethren Levites.
\v 10 The temple therefore of our Lord being founded by the masons, the priests stood in their attire with trumpets: and the Levites the children of Asaph in cymbals, to praise God \f b \fr 3.10 \ft By the ordinance of David, \+it 1. Par. 25.\+it*\f* by the hands of David the king of Israel.
\v 11 And they sang together in hymns, and confession to our Lord: Because he is good, because his mercy is for ever upon Israel. All the people also made a shout with a loud cry in praising our Lord, because the temple of our Lord was founded.
\v 12 Very many also of the Priests and the Levites, and the Princes of the fathers, and the ancients, that had seen the former temple, when they saw this temple founded, \f c \fr 3.12 \ft By their weeping they testified that the new temple was not so excellent as the former. And therefore Aggeus' prophecy \+it (c. 2.)\+it* cannot be understood of this temple, but of the Church of Christ. \+it S. Aug. li. 18. civit. c. 45.\+it*\f* they wept with a loud voice: and many shouting in joy, lifted up their voice.
\v 13 Neither could any man discern the voice of the cry of them that rejoiced, and the voice of the weeping of the people: for one with another the people shouted with a loud cry, and the voice was heard far off.
\c 4
\cd The schismatical Samaritans, because they are not admitted to communicate with the Jews, endeavour to hinder the building of the Temple. 5. Which nevertheless proceedeth all the days of king Cyrus: 7. but is hindered by Artaxerxes, till the second year of Darius.
\p
\v 1 But the enemies of Juda and Benjamin heard that the children of the captivity built a temple to our Lord the God of Israel.
\v 2 And coming to Zorobabel, & the Princes of the fathers, they said to them: Let us build with you, because \f a \fr 4.2 \ft Schismatics and Heretics may not be admitted to communicate in sacrifice with Catholics.\f* even as you, so do we seek your God: Behold we have immolated victims from the days of Asor Haddan the king of Assur, which brought us hither.
\v 3 And Zorobabel said to them, and Josue, and the rest of the Princes of the fathers of Israel: It is not for you and us to build a house to our God, but we ourselves alone will build to the Lord our God, as Cyrus the king of the Persians hath commanded us.
\v 4 It came to pass therefore that the people of the land hindered the hands of the people of Juda, & troubled them in building.
\v 5 And they hired counsellers against them, to destroy their counsel all the days of Cyrus the king of the Persians, and until the reign of Darius the king of the Persians.
\v 6 And in the reign of Assuerus, in the beginning of his reign, they wrote an accusation against the inhabitants of Juda and Jerusalem.
\v 7 And in the days of Artaxerxes, Beselam, Mithridates, & Thabeel, and the rest that were in their counsel writ to Artaxerxes king of the Persians: and the epistle of the accusation was written in Syriac, and was read in the Syrian language.
\v 8 Reum Beelteem, and Samsai scribe wrote one epistle from Jerusalem to Artaxerxes the king, of this tenure:
\v 9 Reum Beelteem, and Samsai scribe, and the rest of their counsellers, the Dineites, and the Apharsathaceites, the Terphaleites, the Apharsaites, the Erchueites, the Babylonians, the Susanecheites, the Dievites, and the Ælamites,
\v 10 and the rest of the Gentiles, which Asenaphar the great and glorious transported: and made them dwell in the cities of Samaria, and in the rest of the countries beyond the River in peace:
\v 11 (this is the copy of the epistle, which they sent to him:) To Artaxerxes the king, thy servants, the men that are beyond the River, send greeting.
\v 12 Be it known to the king, that the Jews, which came up from thee to us, are come into Jerusalem, a rebellious & naughty city, which they build, making the rampires thereof, & repairing the walls.
\v 13 Now therefore be it known to the king, that if that city shall be built, & the walls thereof repaired, they will not give tribute, and toll, and yearly rents, and this damage will come even to the kings.
\v 14 But we mindful of the salt that we have eaten in the palace, and because we count it heinous to see the king's harms, therefore we have sent and certified the king,
\v 15 that thou recount in the Books of the histories of thy fathers, and thou shalt find written in the commentaries, and shalt know that that city is a rebellious city, and hurtful to the kings, and provinces, and battles are raised in it of old time: for the which cause also the city it-self was destroyed.
\v 16 We certify the king, that if that city shall be built, and the walls thereof repaired, thou shalt have no possession beyond the River.
\v 17 The king sent word to Reum Beelteem, and Samsai scribe, and to the rest that were in their counsel inhabitants of Samaria, and to the rest beyond the River, sending greeting and peace.
\v 18 The accusation, which you have sent to us, was openly read before me.
\v 19 and I gave commandment: and they recounted, and have found, that that city of old time rebelleth against the kings, & seditions and battles are raised in it.
\v 20 For there have been also most valiant kings in Jerusalem, which also had dominion over all the country that is beyond the River. They took also tribute, & toll and rents.
\v 21 Now therefore hear the sentence: Prohibit ye those men, that that city be not built, till it perhaps shall be commanded by me.
\v 22 See that you do not negligently accomplish this thing, and by little there grow evil against the kings.
\v 23 Therefore the copy of the edict of Artaxerxes the king was read before Reum Beelteem, and Samsai the scribe, and their counsellers: and they went in haste into Jerusalem to the Jews, and prohibited them with arm and strength.
\v 24 Then was the work of the house of our Lord in Jerusalem intermitted, and was not done until the second year of the reign of Darius the king of the Persians.
\c 5
\cd By the exhortation of Aggæus, and Zacharias, the people proceed in building the Temple. 3. Which their enemies striving to hinder, for decision of the cause, both parties write to king Darius.
\p
\v 1 And there prophesied Aggæus the Prophet, and Zacharias the son of Addo, prophesying to the Jews, that were in Jewry and Jerusalem, in the name of the God of Israel.
\v 2 Then rose up Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and Josue the son of Josedec, and began to build the temple of God in Jerusalem, and with them the Prophets of God helping them.
\v 3 But at the same time there came to them Thathanai, who was Prince beyond the River, and Stharbuzanai, and their counsellers: and said thus to them: Who hath given you counsel to build this house, and to repair the walls?
\v 4 Whereto we answered them, what the names were of the men that were authors of that building.
\v 5 And \f a \fr 5.5 \ft God giving courage to his servants, struck their enemies with terror, & so made them cease from hindering his work as they before intended.\f* the eye of their God was set upon the ancients of the Jews, and they could not inhibit them. And it pleased them that the matter should be referred to Darius, and then they would satisfy against that accusation.
\v 6 The copy of the epistle, which Thathanai Prince of the country beyond the River sent, and Stharbuzanai, and his counsellers the Arphasacheites, which were beyond the River, to Darius the king.
\v 7 The word, which they sent him, was written thus: To Darius the king all peace.
\v 8 Be it known to the king, that we went to Jury, the province, to the house of the great God, which is in building with stone unpolished, and timber is put in the walls: and that work is in building diligently, and groweth in their hands.
\v 9 We therefore demanded of those ancients, and thus we said to them: Who hath given you authority to build this house, and to repair these walls?
\v 10 Yea and their names we asked of them, that we might certify thee: and we writ the names of those men, that are the chief amongst them.
\v 11 And they answered us these words, saying: We are the servants of the God of heaven and earth, and we do build a temple, that was built these many years before, and which a great king of Israel built and set up.
\v 12 But after that our fathers provoked the God of heaven to wrath, he delivered them into the hands of Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon the Chaldee: this house also he destroyed, and his people he transported into Babylon.
\v 13 But in the first year of Cyrus the king of Babylon, Cyrus the king put forth an edict, that this house of God should be built.
\v 14 For the vessels also of the temple of God, of gold and of silver, which Nabuchodonosor had taken out of the temple that was in Jerusalem, and had carried them into the temple of Babylon, Cyrus the king brought forth out of the temple of Babylon, and they were given to Sassabasar so called, whom also he appointed the chief,
\v 15 and said to him: Take these vessels, and go, and put them in the temple, that is in Jerusalem, and let the house of God be built in his place.
\v 16 Then therefore came this Sassabasar, and laid the foundations of the temple of God in Jerusalem, & from that time until now it is in building, & is not yet finished.
\v 17 Now therefore if it seem good to the king, let him search in the king's library, which is in Babylon, whether it hath been commanded by Cyrus the king, that the house of God in Jerusalem should be built, and let him send the king's pleasure concerning this thing unto us.
\c 6
\cd Darius finding in the register, that Cyrus gave license to build the Temple, commandeth that none hinder it: 8. giveth also money towards the charges, and hosts for sacrifice.
\p
\v 1 Then Darius the king commanded, and they searched in the library of the Books, that were laid up in Babylon,
\v 2 and there was found in Ecbatanis, which is a castle in the province Medena, one volume, & there was such a commentary written therein:
\v 3 In the first year of Cyrus the king, Cyrus the king decreed, that the house of God should be built, which is in Jerusalem, in the place where they immolate hosts, & that they lay the foundations supporting the height of threescore cubits, & the breadth of threescore cubits,
\v 4 three rews of stones unpolished, and so many rews of new timber: and the costs shall be given out of the king's house.
\v 5 Yea and the vessels of the temple of God, of gold and of silver, which Nabuchodonosor had taken out of the Temple of Jerusalem, and had brought them into Babylon, let them be restored, and brought back into the temple of Jerusalem unto their place, which also were put in the temple of God.
\v 6 Now therefore Thathanai Prince of the country, that is beyond the River, Stharbuzanai, and your counsellers the Apharsacheites, which are beyond the River, depart far from them,
\v 7 and suffer that temple of God to be made of the Duke of the Jews, and of their ancients, that they may build that house of God in his place.
\v 8 Also there is commandment given from me what must be done of those ancients of the Jews, that the house of God may be built, to wit, that of the king's coffer, that is, of the tributes that are given out of the country beyond the River, the charges be diligently given to those men, lest the work be hindered.
\v 9 And if it shall be necessary, calves also, and lambs, and kids, for holocaust to the God of heaven, wheat, salt, wine, and oil, according to the rite of the Priests that are in Jerusalem, let there be given them day by day, that there be no complain in any thing.
\v 10 And let them offer oblations to the God of heaven, and pray for the life of the king, and of his children.
\v 11 By me therefore there is a decree made: That every man, which shall alter this commandment, there be a beam taken of his house, and set up, and he be fast hanged upon it, and his house be confiscate.
\v 12 And the God, that hath made his name to dwell there, destroy all Kingdoms, and the people that shall extend their hand to resist, and to destroy the house of God, that is in Jerusalem. I Darius have made the decree, which I will to be diligently accomplished.
\v 13 Therefore Thathanai the Prince of the country beyond the River, and Stharbuzanai, and his counsellers, according to that which Darius the king had commanded, so did execute it diligently.
\v 14 And the ancients of the Jews built, and prospered according to the prophecy of Aggeus the Prophet, and of Zacharias the son of Addo: and they built & set up, the God of Israel commanding, and \f a \fr 6.14 \ft The heart of the King is in the hand of our Lord. \+it Proverb. 21.\+it*\f* Cyrus commanding, and Darius, and Artaxerxes the kings of the Persians.
\v 15 And they were finishing this house of God, until the third day of the month of Adar, which is the sixth year of Darius the king.
\v 16 And the children of Israel, the Priests and the Levites, and the rest of the transmigration, made the dedication of the house of God in joy.
\v 17 And they offered in the dedication of the house of God, calves an hundred, rams two hundred, lambs four hundred, buck-goats for the sin of all Israel twelve, according to the number of the tribes of Israel.
\v 18 And they set the Priests in their orders, and the Levites in their courses over the works of God in Jerusalem, as it is written in the Book of Moyses.
\v 19 And the children of Israel of the transmigration made the Phase, the fourteenth day of the first month.
\v 20 For all the Priests and the Levites were purified as it were one man: all clean to immolate the Phase for all the children of the transmigration, and for their brethren the Priests, and themselves.
\v 21 And the children of Israel that were returned from the transmigration, did eat, and all that had separated themselves from the coinquination of the Gentiles of the earth unto them, to seek our Lord the God of Israel.
\v 22 And they made the solemnity of Azymes seven days in joy, because our Lord had made them joyful, and had turned the heart of the king of Assur to them, that he should help their hands in the work of the house of our Lord the God of Israel.
\c 7
\cd Esdras, with many other Priests and Levites, ascendeth to Jerusalem to teach, and assist the people: 11. bringing Artaxerxes' Edict, declareth it to the people, 27. and giveth thanks to God.
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 7.1 \ft The second part. Esdras instructeth the people.\f* after these things in the reign of Artaxerxes king of Persians, Esdras, the son of Saraias, the son of Azarias, the son of Helcias,
\v 2 the son of Sellum, the son of Sadoc, the son of Achitob,
\v 3 the son of Amarias, the son of Azarias, the son of Maraioth,
\v 4 the son of Zarahias, the son of Ozi, the son of Bocci,
\v 5 the son of Abisue, the son of Phinees, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the Priest from the beginning:
\v 6 The same Esdras \f a \fr 7.6 \ft Esdras came with the first from Babylon \+it (2. Esd. 12.)\+it* but returned thither, and now ascended the second time to Jerusalem.\f* came up from Babylon, and he was a quick scribe in the law of Moyses, which our Lord God gave to Israel: and the king gave him according to the hand of our Lord his God upon him all his petition.
\v 7 And there came up of the children of Israel, and of the children of the Priests, and of the children of the Levites, and of the singing men, and of the porters, and of the Nathineites into Jerusalem in the seventh year of Artaxerxes the king.
\v 8 And they came into Jerusalem the fifth month, that is the seventh year of the king.
\v 9 For in the first day of the first month he began to go up from Babylon, and in the first day of the fifth month he came into Jerusalem, according to the good hand of his God upon him.
\v 10 For Esdras prepared his heart to search the law of our God, & to do and to teach in Israel precept & judgment,
\v 11 And this is the copy of the epistle of the edict, which king Artaxerxes gave to Esdras the Priest, the learned scribe, in the words and precepts of our Lord, & his ceremonies in Israel.
\v 12 Artaxerxes the king of kings to Esdras the Priest, the most learned scribe of the law of God of heaven, greeting.
\v 13 It is decreed by me that whomsoever it shall please in my Kingdom, of the people of Israel, and of the Priests and Levites, to go into Jerusalem, let him go with thee.
\v 14 For thou art sent from the face of the king, & of his seven counsellers, that thou mayst visit Jewry & Jerusalem in the law of thy God, which is in thy hand.
\v 15 And that thou mayst carry the silver & gold, which the king & his counsellers have voluntarily offered to the God of Israel, whose tabernacle is in Jerusalem.
\v 16 And all the silver & gold whatsoever thou shalt find in all the Province of Babylon, & the people will offer, & of the Priests that shall voluntarily offer to the house of their God which is in Jerusalem,
\v 17 take freely, and buy diligently of this money calves, rams, lambs, and the sacrifices and libaments of them, and offer them upon the altar of the temple of your God, that is in Jerusalem.
\v 18 Yea and if it shall please thee, and thy brethren to do any thing with the rest of the silver and gold, do ye according to the will of your God.
\v 19 The vessels also, which are given thee for the ministry of the house of thy God, deliver thou in the sight of God in Jerusalem.
\v 20 Yea and other things whereof need shall be for the house of thy God, how much soever is necessary for thee to spend, thou shalt give it out of the treasure and exchequer of the king, and from me.
\v 21 I Artaxerxes the king have appointed and decreed to all the keepers of the Common coffer, that are beyond the River, that whatsoever Esdras the Priest, the scribe of the law of God of heaven, shall ask of you, you give it without delay,
\v 22 unto an hundred talents of silver, and unto an hundred cores of wheat, and unto an hundred bates of wine, and unto an hundred bates of oil, but salt without measure.
\v 23 All that pertaineth to the rite of the God of heaven let it be given diligently in the house of the God of heaven: lest perhaps he be angry against the kingdom of the king, and of his sons.
\v 24 We do you also to understand concerning all the Priests, & Levites, and the singers, and the porters, the Nathineites, and ministers of the house of this God, that you have no authority to put toll and tribute and yearly rents upon them.
\v 25 And thou Esdras according to the wisdom of thy God, which is in thy hand, appoint judges and presidents, that they may judge for all the people, that is beyond the River, that is for them which know the law of thy God, yea and the ignorant teach ye freely.
\v 26 And every one that shall not do the law of thy God, and the law of thy king diligently, there shall be judgement of him, either unto death, or into banishment, or to the confiscation of his substance, or at the least into prison.
\v 27 Blessed be our Lord the God of our fathers, which hath put this in the king's heart, that he would glorify the house of our Lord, which is in Jerusalem,
\v 28 and hath inclined his mercy toward me before the king and his counsellers, and all the mighty princes of the king: and I taking courage by the hand of our Lord my God, which was on me, gathered together out of Israel princes that should go up with me.
\c 8
\cd Esdras reciteth those that came with him from Babylon, 21. the fast which he appointed, 33. and how they brought the holy vessels into the Temple.
\p
\v 1 These therefore are the Princes of the families, and the genealogy of them, that came up with me in the reign of Artaxerxes the king \f a \fr 8.1 \ft This great number which by Esdras' persuasion came from Babylon, signified the great fruit of souls converted from sin by the exhortation of holy preachers. \+it S. Beda li. 2. in Esdr. c. 10.\+it*\f* out of Babylon.
\v 2 Of the children of Phinees, Gersom. Of the children of Ithamar, Daniel. Of the children of David, Hattus.
\v 3 Of the children of Sechenias, the children of Pharos, Zacharias: and with him were numbered an hundred fifty men.
\v 4 Of the children of Phahath Moab, Eleoenai the son of Zareha, and with him two hundred men.
\v 5 Of the children of Sechenias, the son of Ezechiel, and with him three hundred men.
\v 6 Of the children of Adan, Abed the son of Jonathan, and with him fifty men.
\v 7 Of the children of Alam, Isaias the son of A thalia, and with him seventy men.
\v 8 Of the children of Saphatia, Zebedia the son of Michael, and with him eighty men.
\v 9 Of the children of Joab, Obedia the son of Jahiel, and with him two hundred and eighteen men.
\v 10 Of the children of Selomith, the son of Josphia, and with him an hundred sixty men.
\v 11 Of the children of Bebai, Zacharias the son of Bebai: and with him twenty eight men.
\v 12 Of the children of Azgad, Ioanan the son of Eccetan, and with him an hundred and ten men.
\v 13 Of the children of Adonicam, which were the last: and these are their names: Elipheleth, and Jehiel, and Samaias, and with them sixty men.
\v 14 Of the children of Begui, Uthai and Zachur, and with them seventy men.
\v 15 And I gathered them to the river, which runneth down to Ahava, and we tarried there three days: and I sought among the people and among the Priests for the children of Levi, and found none there.
\v 16 Therefore I sent Eliezer, and Ariel, and Semeias, and Elnathan, & Jarib, and another Elnathan, and Nathan, and Zacharias, and Mosollam Princes: and Joiarib, and Elnathan, wise men.
\v 17 And I sent them to Eddo, which is chief in the place of Chasphia, and I did put in their mouth the words that they should speak to Eddo, and his brethren the Nathineites in the place of Chasphia, that they should bring us ministers of the house of our God.
\v 18 And they brought us by the good hand of our God upon us a most learned man of the children of Mocholi the son of Levi the son of Israel, and Sarabias and his sons, and his brethren eighteen,
\v 19 and Hasabias, and with him Isaias of the children of Merari, and his brethren, and his sons twenty.
\v 20 And all the Nathineites, which David gave, and the Princes for the ministries of the Levites, Nathineites two hundred twenty: all these were called by their names.
\v 21 And I proclaimed there \f b \fr 8.21 \ft It sufficeth not to part from Babylon (that is, from sin) but we must also do works of satisfaction, and therefore Esdras here proclaimed an extraordinary fast to those that were come from captivity.\f* a fast beside the River of Ahava, that we might be afflicted before the Lord our God, and might desire of him a right way for us and our children, and all our substance.
\v 22 For I was ashamed to ask the king aid and horsemen, that might defend us from the enemy in the way: because we had said to the king: The hand of our God is upon all them, that seek him in goodness: and his empire and strength, and fury upon all them that forsake him.
\v 23 And we fasted, and besought our God hereby: and it fell out prosperously unto us.
\v 24 And I separated twelve of the chief Priests, Sarabias, and Hasabias, and with them ten of their brethren.
\v 25 And I weighed unto them the silver and gold, and the consecrated vessels of the house of our God, which the king had offered and his counsellers, and his Princes, and all Israel of them that were found:
\v 26 and I weighed in their hands of silver six hundred fifty talents, and vessels of silver an hundred, of gold an hundred talents:
\v 27 and cups of gold twenty, which had a thousand solids, and two vessels of the best shining brass, fair as gold.
\v 28 And I said to them: You are the holy of our Lord, and the vessels are holy, and the silver and gold, that is voluntarily offered to our Lord the God of our fathers:
\v 29 Watch & keep it, until you deliver it by weight before the Princes of the Priests, and of the Levites, & the Princes of the families of Israel in Jerusalem, into the treasure of the house of our Lord.
\v 30 And the Priests and the Levites received the weight of the silver and gold, and of the vessels, to carry it to Jerusalem into the house of our God.
\v 31 We therefore did set forward from the river of Ahava the twelfth day of the first month to go on to Jerusalem: and the hand of our God was upon us, & delivered us from the hand of the enemy, and the lier in wait by the way.
\v 32 And we came to Jerusalem, and we tarried there three days.
\v 33 And in the fourth day the silver was weighed, and the gold, and the vessels in the house of our God by the hand of Meremoth the son of Urias the Priest, and with him Eleazar the son of Phinees, and with them Jozabed the son of Josue, and Noadaia the son of Bennoi Levites,
\v 34 according to the number and weight of all: and all the weight was described at that time.
\v 35 Yea & the children of the transmigration that were come from the captivity, offered holocausts to the God of Israel, calves twelve for all the people of Israel, rams ninety six, lambs seventy seven, buck-goats for sin twelve: all for holocaust to our Lord.
\v 36 And they gave the king's edicts to the Princes, that were overseers for the king, and the dukes beyond the River, and they advanced the people and the house of God.
\c 9
\cd For marriages and other association which the Jews had with Gentiles, Esdras lamenteth, 5. and confessing the people's iniquity, prayeth God to conserve them from utter destruction.
\p
\v 1 And after these things were accomplished, the Princes came to me, saying: The people of Israel, and the Priests and Levites \f a \fr 9.1 \ft Malachias the Prophet complaineth also of this fault, \+it c. 2. v. 11.\+it* threatening God's punishment both to superiors and subjects for not correcting it. \+it v. 12.\+it*\f* are not separated from the people of the lands, and from the abominations of them, to wit, of the Chananeite, and Hetheite, and Pherezeite, and Jebuseite, and of the Ammonites, and Moabites, and the Ægyptians, and Amorrheites.
\v 2 For they have taken of their daughters to them and to their sons, and have mingled the holy seed with the people of the lands. The hand also of the Princes and magistrates hath been first in this transgression.
\v 3 And when I had heard this word, I rent my cloak and my coat, and plucked off the hairs of my head and beard, and sat mourning.
\v 4 And there assembled to me all that feared the God of Israel, for the transgression of them that were come from the captivity, and I sat sorrowful, until the evening sacrifice.
\v 5 And at the evening sacrifice I rose out of my affliction, and my cloak and coat being rent I fell upon my knees, and spread forth my hands to our Lord my God,
\v 6 and said: My God I am confounded and ashamed to lift up my face to thee: because our iniquities are multiplied over our head, and our sins have grown even unto heaven.
\v 7 from the days of our fathers: yea and we ourselves also have sinned grievously unto this day, and in our iniquities have we been delivered, ourselves, and our kings, and our Priests, into the hand of the kings of the lands, and into the sword, and into captivity, and into spoil, and into confusion of countenance, as also at this day.
\v 8 And now as it were a little, and for a moment was our prayer made before the Lord our God, that a remnant might be left us, and \f a \fr 9.8 \ft In respect of their great iniquities Esdras presumeth\f* a nail might be given us in his holy place, and that our God would illuminate our eyes, and would give us a little life in our bondage.
\v 9 Because we are bondmen, and in our bondage our God did not forsake us, but he inclined mercy upon us before the king of the Persians, to give us life, & to advance the house of our God, & to build the desolations thereof, & to give us a hedge in Juda & Jerusalem.
\v 10 And now what shall we say, o our God, after these things? Because we have forsaken thy commandments,
\v 11 which thou hast commanded in the hand of thy servants the Prophets, saying: The land, to the which you enter to possess it, is an unclean land, according to the uncleanness of peoples, and of other lands, by the abominations of them that have filled it from mouth unto mouth in their coinquination.
\v 12 Now therefore give not your daughters to their sons, and their daughters take not for your sons, and do ye not seek their peace, and their prosperity for ever: that you may be strengthened, and may eat the goods that are of the land, and may have your children heirs for ever.
\v 13 And after all things that come upon us in our most wicked works, and our most great sin, because thou our God hast delivered us from our iniquity, and hast given us health as it is at this day,
\v 14 that we shall not turn away, & make frustrate thy commandments, neither should join matrimonies with the peoples of these abominations. Why art thou angry with us unto utter destruction, not to leave us a remnant unto salvation?
\v 15 Lord God of Israel thou art just: because we are left, which should be saved as at this day. Behold we are before thee in our sin, for there can be no standing before thee upon this.
\c 10
\cd Esdras calling the people together commandeth them to dismiss the strange women, which they have married: 14. appointing officers to see it executed. 18. and reciteth those which had married such women.
\p
\v 1 Esdras therefore thus praying, and beseeching, and weeping, and lying before the temple of God, there was gathered to him of Israel an exceeding great company of men and women and children, and the people wept with much lamentation.
\v 2 And Sechenias the son of Jehiel of the children of Ælam answered, and said to Esdras: We have transgressed against our God, and have taken to wives strange women of the peoples of the land: and now if there be penance in Israel upon this,
\v 3 let us make a covenant with the Lord our God, to put away all the wives, and them that are born of them, according to the will of our Lord, and of them that fear the precept of the Lord our God: be it done according to the law.
\v 4 Arise, it is thy part to discern, and we will be with thee: take courage, and do it.
\v 5 Esdras therefore rose up, and adjured the Princes of the Priests and of the Levites, & all Israel, that they should do according to this word, and they sware.
\v 6 And Esdras rose up before the house of God, & went to the chamber of \f a \fr 10.6 \ft Esdras being extraordinarily sent by God to correct the people, repaireth to the high Priest's son, by his authority calleth the people together, and so proceedeth to make reformation. So S. Paul conferred with other Apostles. \+it Gal. 2.\+it*\f* Johanan the son of Eliasib, and entered into it: he did eat no bread, & drank no water: for he mourned for the transgression of them that were come out of the captivity.
\v 7 And there was a proclamation sent in Juda and Jerusalem to all the children of the transgression, that they should assemble together into Jerusalem.
\v 8 And every one that shall not come within three days, according to the counsel of the Princes & ancients, all his substance shall be taken away and himself shall be cast out of the company of the transmigration.
\v 9 There assembled therefore all the men of Juda, and Benjamin into Jerusalem within three days, that is the ninth month, the twentieth day of the month: and all the people sat in the street of the house of God, trembling for the sin, and \f b \fr 10.9 \ft Their sin was punished by over-much rain. \+it v. 13.\+it* And so affliction gave them more feeling of their faults.\f* the rain.
\v 10 And Esdras the Priest arose, and said to them: You have transgressed, and taken strange women to wife, to add upon the sin of Israel.
\v 11 And now give confession to our Lord the God of your fathers, and do his pleasure, and be separated from the peoples of the land, and from your wives the strangers.
\v 12 And all the multitude answered, and said with a loud voice: According to thy word unto us, so be it done.
\v 13 Nevertheless because there is much people, and a time of rain, and we cannot abide to stand without, and it is not a work of one day or two (for we have exceedingly sinned in this thing),
\v 14 let there be Princes appointed in all the multitude: & let all in our cities that have taken strangers to wife, come at set times, and with them the ancients by city and city, and the judges thereof, until the wrath of our God be turned away from us for this sin.
\v 15 Therefore Jonathan the son of Azahel, and Iaazia the son of Thecua were appointed over this, and Mosollam and Sebethai Levites did help them:
\v 16 and the children of the transmigration did so. And Esdras the Priest, and the men, Princes of the families, went into the houses of their fathers, & all by their names, and they sat in the first day of the tenth month to search out the matter.
\v 17 And all the men were fully counted that had taken strangers to wife, unto the first day of the first month.
\v 18 And there were found of the sons of the Priests that had taken strangers to wife. Of the children of Josue the son of Josedec, and his brethren, Maasia, and Eliezer, and Jarib, and Godolia.
\v 19 And they gave their hands to put away their wives, & to offer for their offence a ram of the flock.
\v 20 And of the children of Emmer, Hanani, and Zebedia.
\v 21 And of the children of Harim, Maasia, and Elia, and Semeia, and Jehiel, and Ozias.
\v 22 And of the children of Pheshur, Elionai, Maasia, Ismael, Nathanael, Jozabed, and Elasa.
\v 23 And of the children of the Levites, Jozabed, and Semei, and Celaia, the same is Calita, Phataia, Juda, & Eliezer.
\v 24 And of the singing men Eliasib: and of the porters, Sellum, and Thelem, and Uri.
\v 25 And of Israel, of the children of Pharos, Remeia, and Iezia, and Melchia, and Miamin, and Eliezer, and Melchia, and Banea.
\v 26 And of the children of Ælam, Mathania, Zacharias, and Jehiel, and Abdi, and Jerimoth, and Elia.
\v 27 And of the children of Zethua, Elioenai, Eliasib, Mathania, Jerimuth, and Zabad, and Aziza.
\v 28 And of the children of Bebai, Johanan, Hanania, Zabbai, Athalai.
\v 29 And of the children of Bani, Mosollam, and Melluch, and Adaia, Jasub, and Saal, and Ramoth.
\v 30 And of the children of Phahath Moab, Edna & Chalal, Bananias, and Maasias, Mathanias, Beseleel, Bennui, and Manasse.
\v 31 And of the children of Herem, Eliezer, Josue, Melchias, Semeias, Simeon,
\v 32 Benjamin, Maloch, Samarias.
\v 33 And of the children of Hasom, Mathanai, Mathatha, Zabad, Eliphelet, Iermai, Manasse, Semei.
\v 34 Of the children of Bani, Maaddi, Amram, and Vel,
\v 35 Baneas, and Badaias, Cheliau,
\v 36 Vania, Marimuth, and Eliasib,
\v 37 Mathanias, and Mathanai, and Iasi,
\v 38 and Bani, and Bennui, Semei,
\v 39 and Salmias, and Nathan, & Adaias,
\v 40 and Mechnedebai, Sisai, Sarai.
\v 41 Ezrel, and Selemiau, Semeria,
\v 42 Sellum, A maria, Joseph.
\v 43 Of the children of Nebo, Jehiel, Mathathias, Zabad, Zabina, Ieddu, and Joel, and Banaia.
\v 44 All these had taken strangers to wife, and there were of them that had born \f c \fr 10.44 \ft Amongst other inconveniences of unlawful marriages one is that children are born illegitimate.\f* children.
